{
  "rationale": "Evidence from iteration 1 (entityR2A, race of the kp 3.5, kd 0.6, base 1.3 design): the oval is now solved, with 20/20 finishes at 2.03 s and max_error 0.025. The s-bend improved to 45% finishes, but the slalom still ends off track in all 20 trials (progress 0.30, max_error 0.064, line_loss_events 1.15). The steering-jitter metric on the s-bend rose to 0.37, so there is some oscillation, but the dominant failure is still leaving the tape at sharp bends. Line losses on the slalom show that the tape is leaving the 5-sensor array entirely, not just being corrected too slowly. The array spans only ±0.03 m, which is about the tape half-width plus 5 mm, so a robot sitting 0.03 m off the centre line already has the outer sensors off the tape.\n\nChange: widen the sensor span from ±0.03 m to ±0.04 m (x = -0.04, -0.02, 0, 0.02, 0.04). This keeps tape under at least one sensor when the robot drifts further before line loss, which should cut line-loss events on the slalom and s-bend. Sensor weights stay [-2,-1,0,1,2], so the signal scale is unchanged at the same reading. Controller stays at kp 3.5, kd 0.6, ki 0, base_speed 1.3, last_direction, so this iteration isolates the geometry effect against the iteration 1 baseline. Expected result: slalom line_loss_events drop below 1.0 and max_error stays under 0.06 on more trials, with the oval still finishing near 2.0 s. Risk: a wider array has a coarser signal near the centre (sensors at ±0.02 spacing), which could add mild oscillation on straights, and the wider span may slightly weaken the error estimate on tight bends.\n\nHypotheses: (a) the slalom failures are line losses from the array span, so wider span raises slalom progress; (b) if slalom still fails with max_error near 0.06, the next step is to lower base_speed to about 1.2 only if oval completion_time stays under 3.0 s. Measurements requested: all metrics, so I can compare per-track line_loss_events, max_error and steering_oscillation against iteration 1.",
